Title: Data Segregation Strategy

PostgreSQL data segregation is implemented by isolating each tenant’s information into its own database, ensuring that schemas, tables, and data remain fully separated at the storage and access-control level. This physical separation eliminates the risk of cross-tenant queries and simplifies backup, restore, and lifecycle management per tenant. Complementing this, Keycloak enforces identity and access segregation using realms, where each realm represents a completely isolated security domain with its own users, clients, roles, policies, and authentication flows. By aligning each PostgreSQL database with a corresponding Keycloak realm, the architecture guarantees strict isolation between entities both at the data layer and the identity/security layer, creating a robust multi-tenant foundation with strong boundaries across tenants.